00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:46.600
Strategy Spotlight today centers on how options liquidity actually forms. Weekly options often get dismissed for thin liquidity. The real driver of liquidity is the underlying stock's own market depth rather than the contract's time to expiration.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:02:03.880
Market makers hedge weekly and monthly options the same way against the stock itself. Open interest figures alone do not reveal true tradeability. A monthly option listed 12 months ago can show higher open interest simply because it has had more time to accumulate positions.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:17.780
A weekly listed two weeks ago starts with lower numbers even if the underlying trades millions of shares daily. Today's mixed US equity moves and steady TSX volume make this distinction practical for Canadian investors.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:33.640
Investors who want to use shorter dated contracts inside a TFSA need to avoid widening spreads compare the bid ask spread an average daily volume of the underlying equity against the 20-day norm before entering any weekly option.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:46.680
Platforms such as interactive brokers or quest trade display these metrics directly in the options chain. The approach has historically worked best in high volume names where the stock itself provides the hedge.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:55.720
The main risk is assuming all weeklies behave alike. Low volume underlying will still produce wide spreads regardless of expiration length.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:12.980
Investors who skip the stock volume check often pay more in slippage than the time premium difference between weekly and monthly expirations. Now we turn to the practice investment of the day. This is a simulated trade for educational purposes only. No real money is involved.

