00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:37.660
Now here is something most retail investors get wrong and it can cost real money before the pattern becomes clear.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:44.080
Imagine you want to sell a weekly call on a TSX listed energy name after seeing a sector bounce.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:48.400
Your order fills at a $0.12 spread instead of the usual $0.3.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:58.980
What actually happened is the market makers hedge in the underlying stock moved against them intraday. They widened the quote to cover the risk of holding the position until expiration.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:05.060
Market makers delta hedge every option they write by buying or selling shares of the stock.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:13.960
When the stock's own average daily volume is only 800,000 shares, even modest option flow forces larger price concessions.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:20.420
Check the stock's 20-day average volume and recent bid-ask spread before looking at any option chain.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:25.720
If the stock itself is illiquid, no expiration will magically improve matters.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:35.520
Liquidity therefore flows from the equity market makers ability to offset risk quickly. It does not flow from how many days remain until the option expires.

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:42.640
The common misconception is that longer dated options are always more liquid simply because they have existed longer.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:50.060
The fix is to evaluate liquidity at the stock level first, then choose the expiration that matches your time horizon.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:57.320
Canadian investors inside a TFSA face an extra layer because contribution room is limited.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.120
Paying unnecessary spreads on illiquid underlying compounds the cost of every trade.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:12.080
Checking the underlying volume takes 30 seconds on any platform and prevents the surprise of a 12 cent fill on what looked like a routine weekly.

